    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-revolution">Russian Revolution of 1917</a> several factions sought an independent Ukrainian state. The internationally recognised Ukrainian People's Republic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> faced a Soviet Russia-aligned government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> declared the first Soviet Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> on 24 December 1917, beginning the Ukrainian–Soviet War, part of both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a> and the Ukrainian War of Independence. That republic was dissolved after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-brest-litovsk">Treaty of Brest-Litovsk</a>, when the Bolsheviks were forced out of Ukraine.</p><p>In July 1918 the Communist Party (Bolsheviks) of Ukraine was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-powers">Central Powers</a>' defeat a Provisional Workers' and Peasants' Government was set up at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kursk">Kursk</a> in November 1918. On 10 March 1919 the Third All-Ukrainian Congress of Soviets ratified the constitution of the Ukrainian Socialist Soviet Republic in Kharkiv. Requisitioners from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> met resistance, and from mid-1919 the republic's defence, economy, finance and transport were subordinated to Russia's commissariats; some historians hold that Soviet Ukraine was under Soviet Russia's control from then. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-riga">Peace of Riga</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> held most of Ukraine, western Ukraine going to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. On 30 December 1922 the republic became a founding member of the USSR, its name changed to Ukrainian Soviet Socialist Republic in 1936–1937.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>At the start more than 90% of the workforce were peasants. War <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">Communism</a> and food requisitions devastated the economy: by early 1921 only 4,060 of more than 10,000 industrial enterprises still worked, and a famine of 1921–1923 claimed up to one million lives. The New Economic Policy brought recovery, until from 1925 the regime turned to industrialisation and collectivisation. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> of 1932–1933, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> exported over a million tonnes of grain, the famine caused a direct loss of life estimated at between 2.6 million and 10 million. Between 1928 and 1937 the republic's industry grew at record rates; it became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s second biggest producer of pig iron and fourth in the world in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>.</p><p>After the war, the Fourth Five-Year Plan restored industry: by 1955 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> was producing 2.2 times more than in 1940, the largest per-capita producer in Europe of pig iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>. Agriculture lagged, and growth slowed to 3.5% in the Eleventh Five-Year Plan (1981–1985). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reservoir">reservoirs</a>, natural gas and a nuclear programme supplied energy. Peasants' private plots grew more than half of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potatoes</a>. The population reached 51,706,746 in 1989, and by 1979 53% of ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> lived in towns; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> grew from 1.1 million in 1959 to 2.1 million in 1979.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic's defence was managed by the Soviet Armed Forces; a 1944 clause allowing it a standing army of its own was never fulfilled. Its land was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> of some of the largest battles of the German-Soviet War from June 1941. Soviet armies were defeated at the Battle of Brody, over 100,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> soldiers were encircled at Uman, and in the ring east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> 453,000 Soviet soldiers and officers were captured, by Soviet data; Kyiv was abandoned on 18 September 1941. A failed counteroffensive near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> in 1942 cost 171,000 dead and 240,000 captured, and by 27 July 1942 all of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> was occupied. The retreating Soviet forces evacuated over 550 enterprises and destroyed what they could not move, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a> Hydroelectric Station.</p><p>Soviet partisans under Tymofiy Strokach and nationalist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> of the Ukrainian Insurgent Army (UPA) both fought the occupiers; in July 1943 UPA forces under Dmytro Klyachkivsky carried out ethnic cleansing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>. Soviet armies entered Kyiv on 6 November 1943 and expelled German troops from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Transcarpathia</a> by 28 October 1944. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> then fought the UPA, killing over 103,000 rebels in 1944–1945; its commander Roman Shukhevych was killed in 1950, and the insurgency faded after 1954.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ukrainian SSR was governed by the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, the republican branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-the-soviet-union">Communist Party of the Soviet Union</a>; real power lay with the party's politburo and First Secretary, and the regime can be characterised as a form of party <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>. Legislative authority lay first with the Congress of Soviets, whose Central Executive Committee was long headed by Grigory Petrovsky, and then with a Supreme Soviet of 450 deputies, which met only a few weeks a year; elections every five years were symbolic. The republic was divided into oblasts, 25 by 1991, and 490 raions, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a> as cities with special status.</p><p>The secret police, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> and its successor the GPU, acted as a "state within a state", staging annual show trials. In the 1920s korenizatsiya took the form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainisation">Ukrainization</a>, halted in 1932–1933. Stalinist terror struck National Communists, specialists and the intelligentsia; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk">Mykola Skrypnyk</a> and Mykola Khvylyovy were driven to suicide, and hundreds of thousands were executed. After Stalin, Aleksey Kirichenko became in 1953 the first ethnic Ukrainian to lead the party since the 1920s; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petro-shelest">Petro Shelest</a>, removed in 1972, was followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volodymyr-shcherbytsky">Volodymyr Shcherbytsky</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic's borders changed many times. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molotov-ribbentrop-pact">Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact</a> it gained Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1939 and lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> in 1940, reaching 560,000 sq km; in 1945 it gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-ruthenia">Carpathian Ruthenia</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>. Population exchanges moved 810,400 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> to Poland and 482,900 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> from it between 1944 and 1946. In 1954 the Russian SFSR transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. In the 1920s it had ceded Belgorod, Taganrog and Starodub to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>The republic also had a place in the world. In the early 1920s it signed treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> and Latvia and had its own diplomats until 1923. From 1945 it sat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> as a founding member, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> two extra votes in the General Assembly, and joined bodies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> to the International Atomic Energy Agency. Its culture lived under repression: more than half the members of the Writers' Union were repressed between 1934 and 1938, churches fell from about 10,000 to 850 by 1937, and the Ukrainian Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> was abolished in 1946. Yet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> gave the world Alexander Dovzhenko's Arsenal and Earth and the poetic cinema of Sergei Parajanov, and dissidents such as Ivan Dziuba, Viacheslav Chornovil and the Ukrainian Helsinki Group of 1976 kept up opposition.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Perestroika reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> late under the conservative Shcherbytsky, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernobyl-disaster">Chernobyl disaster</a> of 1986, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russification">Russification</a> and stagnation turned many against Soviet rule. Glasnost brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a> into the open. Civic groups grew: the Ukrainian Helsinki Union under Levko Lukianenko in 1988, the People's Movement of Ukraine (Rukh) in September 1989, which by October 1990 had 633,000 activists. In October 1989 the Law on Languages made Ukrainian the state language. On 21 January 1990 hundreds of thousands formed a human chain from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivano-frankivsk">Ivano-Frankivsk</a>.</p><p>On 16 July 1990 the Supreme Soviet adopted the Declaration of State <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">Sovereignty</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonid-kravchuk">Leonid Kravchuk</a> became its head. A students' hunger strike in October 1990 forced out the prime minister Vitaliy Masol. When the August Coup began in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 19 August 1991, Kravchuk hesitated, then defended sovereignty; on 24 August the Supreme Soviet adopted the Act of Declaration of Independence of Ukraine, ending the Ukrainian SSR, and the Communist Party of Ukraine was banned days later. On 1 December 1991 92.3% voted for independence and Kravchuk was elected president with 62 percent. On 8 December he signed the Belovezh Accords, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> dissolved on 26 December 1991. Ukraine became its legal successor and kept its UN seat.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ukrainian SSR was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s second republic in population and economic power, created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> against the Ukrainian People's Republic and governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>. It suffered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holodomor">Holodomor</a>, the Stalinist terror and the German occupation, grew into an industrial heartland, and ended in 1991 when its Supreme Soviet declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a> independent.</em></p>

